Target installed. crates.io API returned something (the second curl output got cut? Actually output only shows the 403 for static, and the crates.io api head -c 200 maybe returned nothing). Let me test cargo add / fetch connectivity properly later. The 403 on static.crates.io/crates/windows/ is expected (directory listing forbidden). crates.io index reachability is what matters.

Let me decide: I'll avoid external crates to keep it robust and fast, using raw FFI. That fully satisfies "platform-specific import libraries for Windows API bindings" and doesn't depend on crates.io.

Actually, let me reconsider one more time. Using raw FFI is a legitimate "Windows API binding". Good. Let me create the project.

`cargo init` in /work. But cargo init might complain the directory is not empty (it's fine, it will just add files). It will create Cargo.toml, src/main.rs, .gitignore.

Let me init with name. Then overwrite main.rs and Cargo.toml, create .cargo/config.toml.

Let me write the project files. I'll design main.rs:

```rust
#![windows_subsystem = "windows"]  // maybe not, keep console for test

use std::ffi::OsStr;
use std::iter::once;
use std::os::windows::ffi::OsStrExt;
```

Wait, for x86_64-pc-windows-gnu, the std is available, so `std::os::windows` works. But we're cross-compiling; cfg(windows) is true for the target.

Bindings:
```rust
type HWND = *mut core::ffi::c_void;
type UINT = u32;
type BOOL = i32;

#[link(name = "user32")]
extern "system" {
    fn MessageBoxW(hwnd: HWND, text: *const u16, caption: *const u16, utype: UINT) -> i32;
}
```

Also kernel32:
```rust
#[link(name = "kernel32")]
extern "system" {
    fn GetModuleHandleW(name: *const u16) -> *mut c_void;
}
```

main:
```rust
fn to_wide(s: &str) -> Vec<u16> { s.encode_utf16().chain(once(0)).collect() }

fn main() {
    unsafe {
        let text = to_wide("Hello from x86_64-pc-windows-gnu!");
        let caption = to_wide("Rust Win32");
        MessageBoxW(std::ptr::null_mut(), text.as_ptr(), caption.as_ptr(), 0);
    }
}
```

But note: when running under wine not available. We just compile.

I want to also make sure it demonstrates the linker needs import libs libuser32.a, libkernel32.a from MinGW. Good.

Should I add `#![windows_subsystem = "windows"]`? If set, no console; MessageBox still works. But for a console test it's fine either way. Let me not set it, so it's a console app that pops a message box; actually a console + messagebox is fine. Keep default console.

Now .cargo/config.toml:
```toml
[build]
target = "x86_64-pc-windows-gnu"

[target.x86_64-pc-windows-gnu]
linker = "x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc"
```

Hmm, setting `[build] target` makes `cargo build` default to the cross target. That's convenient for "a project cross-compiled for...". But if someone builds on Windows, it's fine (their default linker maybe present). I'll set it.

Should I also set `runner`? wine not installed. Skip. Maybe add commented runner for wine.

Now, there's a concern: The linker needs to be on PATH. In .cargo/config.toml I can't set PATH. But I can add `rustflags` to pass `-L` for native libs? Not needed; gcc finds sysroot relative.

But wait: will rustc's default linker name for windows-gnu be `x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc`? If so, `linker` line is redundant but explicit. Let me verify rustc target spec. I'll just set it explicitly.

Also, rustc may pass `-lmsvcrt` etc. Fine.

Let me create files. Use cargo init first to get .gitignore. Then edit.

Let me run cargo init.

